    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1309">

                <pb facs="1181" n="923" sameAs="frus:frus1915Supp/pg923"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 125.187/2</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The</hi>
                    
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Secretary of State</hi>
                    
                    <hi rendition="#italic">to the Ambassador in Austria-Hungary</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                    >Penfield</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-12-23T17:00:00-05:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">December 23, 1915, 5 p. m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1052. Your 1017 and 1036.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d1309-f1"/> Inasmuch as there now
                    appear to be practically no American or foreign interests to be cared for in
                    Belgrade, Young should arrange sealing of archives and for safekeeping of
                    Consulate and then proceed to Vienna where he has been temporarily assigned in
                    Consulate General subject to such services as Embassy may require. Detail is,
                    however, only temporary. Belgrade seals should be deposited Vienna
                    Consulate.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
